something that you'll find only on cbs  mornings oprah is in winfrey announcing  her latest pick for her book club and  this is a hint it's a new memoir from a  woman who stole our hearts on tv on the  big screen and on the stage  oprah joins us joined us recently along  with the author remotely from oprah's  porch in hawaii  oprah start us off with this because you  really don't pick many memoirs for your  book club but this one you made an  exception why why did you choose this  particular memoir well it wasn't even an  exception gail i have to tell you that i  read the first sentence and then the  first paragraph and i knew that this was  going to be a book club and let me tell  you what it is and the reason why it's  called  finding  me  by viola david look at the cover just  let us sit at that look at that cover  for just a second it's so striking  what's striking is this biola that we  know and let me tell you  anybody who's loved viola davis you were  a big fan of how to get away with murder  you've watched over the years let me you  do not know viola davis  you don't know viola davis until you  until you meet this girl gail have you  seen this no i haven't seen any of this  davis there ladies and gentlemen let me  introduce viola davis  bravo  bravo viola davis  viola davis congratulations  what did you think when you found out  that you were an oprah book club pick  i'm curious  i flipped out you did i am not going to  lie the fact that she received it yes  the fact that she got it you know a lot  of times you don't think people are  going to get your story especially when  when you've had it bound up inside you  for so long and there's so much shame  attached to it oprah what stood out to  you about this book  well what spoke to me is that you know i  i also grew up poor but i say i bow to  your poorness because yes  i don't know anybody with a story as poe  is this  who managed to not just poor but  colorism racism sexism violence sexual  abuse everything  and to find a way  to literally fight your way out to have  that thing inside you that says  uh i've been denied but i will not live  the rest of my life being denied i mean  i think that there there's so much hope  and so much power in her story that you  know she has done exactly what uh  writing a great memoir does you you let  your words uh embolden other people  viola you start the book by saying i was  a spunky sassy mess i love that you were  all so competitive you were also shy  prime picking for bullies but let's talk  about your bullies for just a second  because these people were just they  weren't just i'm going to beat you up  i'm going to kick you but they were  cruel what did they do  well well they would chase me every  single day after school every day  with every single day and i was a really  fast runner and  with the potty mouth yeah i say yes yes  but um  but the eight or nine boys they would  pick up anything they could find on the  side of the road but here's the thing  with them they're just an extension of  what central falls was at the time  um but more importantly what america was  at the time  we were the only black family in first  black family in central falls and a  couple of other cape verdeans moved in  or whatever  i think it was their response to my  blackness yeah  it's them spitting out the very thing  about america that america even hated at  the time  what that does to a little girl  to be told that you are nothing you are  worthless and you are ugly and that's  what that's what hurt me so much biola  and it's what hurt me too yeah  because i believe them you believe them  and you know as you know they tell you  to fight my mom told me to fight bala  don't come home yes until you beat their  butt yes you don't come home and so i  fought and i fought them and i made them  stop  but i didn't know  how to fight that feeling inside that  i'm not what you say that i am you know  you also write something that most  people don't talk about colorism well  there's been something really deeply  interjected in our  uh in our culture  about beauty  and classic beauty and womanhood  and that paper bag test  did a number on us  and we created that  and the darker you are from the paper  bag the more  less intelligent you are  the ugly less attractive you are yes  and here's the thing  the most important thing this is the  biggest issue i have the less valuable  you are  beauty is attached to a value  and if you don't have it you have  nothing  once i got older into my 40s  i started asking a really really  important question which is  why  who said yes i'm not beautiful  and then  something  happened within all of that this little  flicker this little voice that said  i have the power to reject that  that is an opinion  that is not the truth  oprah what did you think about viola's  comments about colorism well i thought  what what i thought was so liberating  was how she was able to express in  finding me  how  how to get away with murder yeah really  changed her life yes being able to bring  beauty  sexuality a sense of value  importance and power to that character  and to be able to express that to the  world through annelise yeah and and  speaking of playing a role i i like  viola how you said you looked at cicely  tyson and you thought oh  i want to do that  and the way you looked up to cicely  tyson is the way so many people now look  up to you  they're looking up to you saying i want  to be like viola davis i want to do that  what does that mean to you  it means everything to me  you know i'll tell you when i looked  into the eyes of so many young  especially dark-skinned young girls  which that's a whole other narrative  right there yeah yeah  and they feel like i've given them  permission to see themselves  as a thing of beauty as a thing of  possibilities  absolutely it gives me a different kind  of joy than i get from even my oscar my  emmy and all of those other  awards and accomplishments